@Items/inventory
The items/inventory editor menu allows editing two things, which are the
item definitions and the inventory lists.

!dl;Item definition list
!de;Item definition editor
!inv;Inventory
!ip;Inventory properties
!is;Item slot

:dl;Item definition list
On this screen, you can select an item definition to edit. You can also add
a new item definition. Item definition 0 cannot be edited, since 0 means the
lack of an item.

:de;Item definition editor
You can edit the following parameters:

þ Name: The visible name of the item. This name can be used for display
during the game and can also be used to refer to this item in scripts. The
name cannot be empty.

þ Class: The class number of the item, from 0 to 127. You can define your
own item classes and can use them to group items by classes.

þ Description: A description of the item, which is only used for display,
if you have defined a part of the item window to display descriptions or
if descriptions are displayed separately. The description is not required.

þ Element: The element number, which is one of the element numbers from
the element definition menu. The default is 255.

þ Color: The colour to use with the element. Set this to zero if you are
not using the colour.

þ Character: This is actually the same as the "parameter" field, but it
is a different representation in the editor. Editing either field will
affect both of them.

þ Parameter: The parameter to use with the element. Set this to zero if you
are not using the parameter. (You can also use this for your own use if you
just want a extra 8-bit number, if the specified element doesn't need it.)

þ Weight: The weight of this item. The total weight of all items in an
inventory (when each weight is multiplied by the corresponding quantity
of that item) cannot exceed the inventory's strength.

þ Max heap: The maximum number of this kind of item that is allowed in a
single inventory slot. The inventory may also have a max heap setting which
also limits it; the smaller limit is used. This does not prevent having
multiple heaps of the same kind of items within an inventory list.

þ Appearance name: An appearance name, e.g. "red potion" or "blue ring",
that does not describe what the item does but only what it appears to be.
You can leave this blank if it is not used in your game world.

þ Script: The script for the item. The program for the game world may load
and execute the script of an item when the item is invoked, and may have
labels (specific to the world) for various functions of the items. You may
start with @! to use a script library. (The script is not required.)

þ Price, ext3, ext4, ext5: These fields do not have any inherent meaning;
you can use them for your own use.

þ Flags: There are standard flags and custom flags. The custom flags have no
inherent use and you can use them for your own use. See the below section
for the working of standard flags.

:df;Item definition flags
The standard flags are as follows:

þ Not discardable: Prevents the player from discarding this item when the
item window is displayed. This should be set for a "key item".

þ Single heap: Prevents multiple heaps of this kind of item from occuring
within a single inventory list. If the max heap limit has been reached,
then no more of that item can be added to that inventory.

þ Hide quantity: Hides the quantity of the item in the item window. This
flag should be set for a unique "key item".

þ Unidentified: This flag does nothing by itself, but may be useful in
combination with your own codes in order to change the display of an item
which has not yet been identified by the player. The value of this flag
can be modified at run time, and will be saved in the save game file.

þ Do not randomize: Prevents randomization from being applied to this
item definition, even if the other criteria are met. If you do not define
any criteria for randomization of item definitions, then this flag is not
necessary and has no effect.

þ Hilight: Causes the hilight flag of the item slot to automatically become
set if #GIVEITEM or IGIV is used to give this item into a vacant slot.
(This does not affect slots that are already occupied, nor does it affect
IPUT, IMOV, etc.) See the explanation in the below section about attributes
of item slots for more details about the use of this flag.

:inv;Inventory
You can define any number of inventory lists. The name must be four hex
digits for a inactive inventory, or X followed by one digit 0 to 7 for a
active inventory. Only active inventories are used directly at run time,
but it is possible to copy between active and inactive at run time.
!is;Item slot

Each inventory list has a number of slots, as well as properties.
!ip;Inventory properties

:is;Item slot
An item slot has the following attributes:

þ Item: The item number. Specify zero if this slot is vacant.

þ Quantity: How many of this item is in this slot. This should be set to
at least one, if there is any of this item.

þ Ext0, Ext1, Ext2: These have no inherent use, and can be used for your
own use. Ext0 is 32-bits and the others are 16-bits.

þ In use: Prevents this item from being discarded, and prevents this slot
from being used when giving or taking items, unless it is deliberately
tring to give/take items that are in use.

þ Fixed: Prevents this item from being moved to a different slot or from
being discarded. If the quantity is reduced to zero, this slot will still
remain here instead of being cleared.

þ Hidden: Causes this item slot to not be displayed in a item menu. (This
does not prevent it from being displayed in the editor.) If this item or
inventory does not have "single heap" flag, then giving a item will not
cause it to add to this heap unless deliberately giving hidden items.

þ Ignore: Many operations such as give, take, find, and count, will ignore
this item. It is also ignored for calculating total weight. This does not
prevent the item from being displayed, discarded, moved, and activated;
you can use other flags to control that.

þ Hilight: This flag affects the display of the item slot in a item menu,
if the hilight colour has been defined for that item menu. It has no other
inherent meaning, so you can use it for your own use as well.

þ Custom flags: Flags that you can define for your own use.

A slot is considered to be "vacant" if the item number is zero and none
of the boolean flags are set. Slots that are not vacant are "occupied".

:ip;Inventory properties
The properties of each inventory list are:

þ Max heap: The maximum quantity per slot. If more items of the same
kind would be added and they do not fit in one slot, another vacant
slot will be used instead.

þ Strength: The maximum total weight of all items. Items cannot be added
if it would cause the sum of the product of the quantity times the weight
of an object in each slot to exceed the strength.

þ Ignore max heap: If selected, then the max heap for the item definition
will be ignored, although the max heap of the inventory is still used. If
not selected, then whichever max heap value is lower will be used.

þ Single heap per item: Disallows adding more of one item into this
inventory list if there is already a slot with this item that has already
reached the max heap for that item.

þ Ignore weight: Causes the weight and strength to be ignored when adding
items to this inventory list.

þ User0, User1, User2, User3: Boolean flags with no inherent meaning.
